<h1>How to Calculate Gratuity for Resignation and Termination in UAE (2026 Guide)</h1>

<p>
Employees working in the United Arab Emirates often want to understand how their end-of-service benefits are calculated when they leave a job. Whether you resign voluntarily or your employer terminates your contract, gratuity can form an important part of your final settlement. Knowing the latest rules can help you estimate what you may receive and avoid confusion during the exit process.
</p>

<p>
In this guide, we explain how to calculate gratuity for resignation and termination in the UAE, what factors affect your payout, and how you can estimate your amount using a reliable <a href="https://gratuity-calculator-uae.ae/">end of service calculator</a>.
</p>

<h2>What Is Gratuity in the UAE?</h2>

<p>
Gratuity is an end-of-service benefit paid by employers to eligible employees after completing a certain period of employment. It is governed by MOHRE regulations and applies to most private-sector workers in the UAE.
</p>

<p>This amount is generally based on:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Basic salary</li>
  <li>Length of service</li>
  <li>Type of contract</li>
  <li>Reason for leaving (resignation or termination)</li>
</ul>

<p>
Employees can estimate their dues using online tools that simplify the process and help calculate expected settlement accurately.
</p>

<h2>Is Gratuity Different for Resignation and Termination?</h2>

<p>
Yes. In the UAE, gratuity eligibility usually depends on whether the employee resigns voluntarily or is terminated by the employer.
</p>

<h3>Resignation</h3>

<p>
When an employee chooses to leave the company by submitting resignation, gratuity is usually payable if they have completed at least one year of continuous service.
</p>

<h3>Termination</h3>

<p>
If an employer ends the contract, the employee may still be entitled to full end-of-service benefits, provided the termination is not due to serious misconduct under UAE labour regulations.
</p>

<p>
The legal framework for this is outlined under <a href="https://gratuity-calculator-uae.ae/labour-law/">labour law UAE</a>, which explains employee rights in detail.
</p>

<h2>Basic Formula for Gratuity Calculation in UAE</h2>

<p>
The standard gratuity formula in the UAE is based on the employee’s basic salary, excluding allowances such as housing, transport, or bonuses.
</p>

<div class="formula">
Gratuity = (Basic Salary ÷ 30) × Eligible Days
</div>

<p>Eligible days are generally:</p>

<ul>
  <li>21 days’ basic salary per year for the first 5 years</li>
  <li>30 days’ basic salary per year for service beyond 5 years</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Calculate Gratuity for Resignation</h2>

<p>If you resign from your job, here is the usual process:</p>

<h3>Step 1: Determine Basic Salary</h3>

<p>
Only your monthly basic salary is considered. For example, if your total salary is AED 8,000 but your basic salary is AED 5,000, gratuity will be calculated on AED 5,000.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: Calculate Years of Service</h3>

<p>
The number of complete years and partial months served are included in the final amount.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: Apply Formula</h3>

<p>For example:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Basic salary = AED 5,000</li>
  <li>Service period = 3 years</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
Daily basic salary: 5000 ÷ 30 = 166.67
</div>

<div class="formula">
Gratuity: 166.67 × (21 × 3) = AED 10,500
</div>

<p>
So the employee may receive approximately AED 10,500.
</p>

<h2>How to Calculate Gratuity for Termination</h2>

<p>
Termination calculations usually follow the same core formula, but the employee may also be entitled to:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Unpaid salary</li>
  <li>Leave encashment</li>
  <li>Notice period compensation</li>
  <li>Other final settlement dues</li>
</ul>

<p>For instance:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Basic salary = AED 6,000</li>
  <li>Service = 6 years</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula">
First 5 years: (6000 ÷ 30) × (21 × 5) = AED 21,000
</div>

<div class="formula">
6th year: (6000 ÷ 30) × 30 = AED 6,000
</div>

<div class="formula">
Total gratuity: AED 27,000
</div>

<h2>Important Factors That Affect Gratuity</h2>

<p>
Several factors can change the final gratuity amount:
</p>

<h3>1. Type of Employment Contract</h3>

<p>
Contract type can influence resignation terms, notice period, and final settlement. It is important to review your employment documents and your official <a href="https://gratuity-calculator-uae.ae/labour-card/">labour card UAE</a> details before calculating dues.
</p>

<h3>2. Basic Salary Changes</h3>

<p>
If your basic salary changed during employment, the latest basic salary is typically used.
</p>

<h3>3. Service Less Than One Year</h3>

<p>
Employees who worked for less than one year usually are not entitled to gratuity.
</p>

<h3>4. Misconduct Cases</h3>

<p>
Employees terminated under serious disciplinary violations may lose gratuity entitlement depending on the case.
</p>

<h2>Resignation vs Termination: Key Difference</h2>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Factor</th>
    <th>Resignation</th>
    <th>Termination</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Who ends contract</td>
    <td>Employee</td>
    <td>Employer</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Gratuity eligibility</td>
    <td>Usually after 1 year</td>
    <td>Usually after 1 year</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Additional compensation</td>
    <td>Limited</td>
    <td>May include notice/compensation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Final settlement</td>
    <td>Standard</td>
    <td>Can include extra dues</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>
Understanding this difference helps employees plan their finances before leaving a job.
</p>

<h2>Can You Calculate Gratuity Online?</h2>

<p>
Yes. Manual calculations can be confusing, especially if you have:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>More than 5 years of service</li>
  <li>Salary revisions</li>
  <li>Partial months worked</li>
  <li>Termination compensation</li>
</ul>

<p>
Using an online calculator is often the easiest way to estimate your entitlement accurately. It provides quick results based on salary, joining date, and exit date.
</p>

<h2>What Is Included in Final Settlement?</h2>

<p>
Apart from gratuity, final settlement may include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Last salary payment</li>
  <li>Unused annual leave compensation</li>
  <li>Notice period payment</li>
  <li>Overtime dues</li>
  <li>Pending allowances</li>
  <li>Ticket allowance (if applicable by contract)</li>
</ul>

<p>
This means your final settlement may be higher than gratuity alone.
</p>

<h2>Common Questions About Gratuity in UAE</h2>

<h3>Do I get gratuity if I resign after 1 year?</h3>

<p>
Yes, in most cases employees become eligible after completing one continuous year of service.
</p>

<h3>Is gratuity calculated on full salary?</h3>

<p>
No. It is usually calculated only on basic salary.
</p>

<h3>Can employer refuse gratuity?</h3>

<p>
An employer generally cannot refuse gratuity if you meet legal eligibility conditions, unless termination involves serious legal misconduct.
</p>

<h3>Is gratuity taxable in UAE?</h3>

<p>
Currently, gratuity is generally not subject to personal income tax in the UAE.
</p>

<h2>Tips Before Resigning or Accepting Termination</h2>

<p>
Before leaving your job, employees should:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Check employment contract</li>
  <li>Confirm final basic salary</li>
  <li>Review notice period</li>
  <li>Verify leave balance</li>
  <li>Keep a copy of salary slips</li>
  <li>Calculate expected gratuity in advance</li>
</ul>

<p>
This helps avoid disputes and ensures you receive the correct amount.
</p>

<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

<p>
Understanding how to calculate gratuity for resignation and termination in the UAE is essential for every employee. Your end-of-service benefit depends mainly on your basic salary, years of service, and whether you resigned or were terminated.
</p>

<p>
Because calculations can vary depending on contract details and settlement components, using an online calculator is often the fastest option. It saves time and gives a close estimate of what you may receive at the end of employment.
</p>

<p>
If you are planning to leave a job in the UAE in 2026, reviewing your contract, checking your labour records, and estimating your gratuity beforehand can help you make informed financial decisions.
</p>

